WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.260
STEPHANIE WONG: One
of the first problems

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.940
you have to tackle when building
a cloud-based application,

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.570
whether it handles
HR or e-commerce,

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.570
is where to run your code.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:10.650
And since each
cloud-based application

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.840
has needs specific
to its environment,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.690
Google Cloud Platform offers
a diverse set of options

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.560
to run your code on.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.950
But then there's a new
problem, figuring out

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.810
what option is right for
your specific use case.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.230
So join us as we explore
some of the options

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.860
that Google offers for compute.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.200
Let's work through this from
the most abstracted options

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.520
to the least.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.030
First, if you're a
mobile developer,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.030
then you might not be
looking for the full suite

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.262
of configurable compute options.

00:00:37.262 --> 00:00:38.720
Most of the time
you want something

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.510
that does storage,
notifications, and syncing,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.240
but with the least amount of
server-side code possible.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.580
For that, you should
check out Firebase.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.730
It offers a plethora of options,
including Firebase functions

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.850
as a service, database
hosting, and storage,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.710
all built with mobile
developers in mind,

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.902
so you get a lot of power
for a little bit of code.

00:00:57.902 --> 00:01:00.110
And that's why Firebase base
is a common first choice

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.630
for mobile and HTML developers.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.910
But if your application
is more complex,

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.520
and you do need
compute, then there

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.500
are other options available.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.750
Let's say your application
scales beyond mobile,

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.260
and you still want to minimize
the amount of server-side code.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.900
Cloud Functions might
be the right choice.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.200
Applications that are
mainly event-driven, say,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.090
your accounting department,
getting an email,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.490
every time your
customer uploads a PDF

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.160
can fit into Cloud Functions
and run just the event

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.250
logic you need without having
to maintain the structure that

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.742
hosts and scales them.

00:01:33.742 --> 00:01:36.200
A second clue to understanding
whether Cloud Functions will

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.770
meet your needs is
in the name itself.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.400
Picture your code for a second.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.920
Does it make sense
to talk about it

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.800
as a bunch of fairly
independent functions?

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.800
Think of Cloud Functions as
a glue connecting your APIs

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.000
together.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.540
With Cloud Functions, you
get full access to our APIs

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.150
and only pay for the time
your functions are running.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.550
Now, having your
application split up

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:02.570
between independent functions
may not always be a great idea.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.050
Like, maybe your application is
too intertwined to spool apart,

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:10.310
or it's filled with legacy
code and too large to refactor.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.133
Either way, you're still
covered thanks to App Engine.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.800
App Engine lets you
run your application

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.870
without having to make
each part available

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.760
as an independent function.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.130
It also supports a
wide range of run times

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.110
and long running
processes, making

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.710
App Engine a flexible fit to
host your complex applications.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.680
When you think of App Engine,
think fast auto scaling.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.560
Let's say you're
running a brand new app,

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.350
and you don't know how demand
is going to fluctuate over

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:41.510
the long term, or maybe you have
a spikey load where usage jumps

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.760
up really high for
short periods of time,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.190
like an advertising
landing page.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:49.850
For you, having consistent
access to a fixed capacity

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.560
doesn't really make sense.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.630
With App Engine, you
pay for the cycle

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.970
so you don't have to guess.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.400
And no matter how
quickly users adopt,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.410
App Engine always keeps up.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.290
So if you want to get your idea
to market fast and be totally

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.080
ready when you're app becomes
a crowd favorite overnight,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.730
App Engine is your best bet.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.000
Now, until recently, if
you wanted the advantages

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.220
of running your
app in containers,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.890
you had to bid farewell to
some of App Engine's most

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:19.400
beloved features, like
[INAUDIBLE] auto scaling.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.340
But thanks to a fourth
option, Cloud Run,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.110
you don't have to anymore.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.810
Cloud Run brings server-less
principles and containers

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:27.950
together.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.750
With our fully
hosted back end, it

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.310
supports the
server-less principles

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.460
of pay for what you
use and quick scaling

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.680
and adds the flexibility
of running anything

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.960
you can run in a container.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.820
Another factor
that can and should

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.100
influence which compute
option you choose

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.820
is, of course, the size
of your operations team.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.920
All of the options
we've discussed so far

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.190
are designed to work
well without one.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.880
With Cloud Run,
it really depends

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.710
on how you plan to use it.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.510
You can think of Cloud
Run as a platform

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.550
for running your
opt containers that

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.110
can be as managed as you want.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.890
You can let it handle tasks like
monitoring application health

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:05.930
and automatically
troubleshooting,

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.850
but you don't have to.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:11.150
Now, if you do already have
some designated team members who

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.520
can oversee tasks like
provisioning, compute,

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.680
and monitoring health,
but you're still

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.870
running your applications
in containers,

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.750
Google Kubernetes Engine
might be a better fit.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:23.640
For those who are new
or need a refresher,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.450
GKE groups containers that
make up an application

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:30.110
into logical units for easy
management and discovery.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.600
For a little more
context, almost everybody

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.960
who decides to use GKE has
already settled on Kubernetes

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.320
as their orchestration system,
and there's just deciding

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.418
how and where to run it.

00:04:41.418 --> 00:04:43.460
Maybe you want to run a
single kind of container,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.210
but on a bunch of VMs and have
it scale up and down as quickly

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.370
as possible, or
you've got a bunch

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.545
of microservices in containers
that all need to work together.

00:04:53.545 --> 00:04:54.920
Sometimes you may
also be looking

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.190
to offload parts of the
management to a provider, too.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:59.960
Remember that
Kubernetes Engine can

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.080
be configured to spin up
new containers automatically

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.030
to grow and shrink
with user demand,

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.928
so you're still going
to get scalability.

00:05:07.928 --> 00:05:10.220
Where an important point of
difference comes into play,

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:11.960
though, is pricing model.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.140
With Google Kubernetes Engine as
well as Google Compute Engine,

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.620
which we'll talk about in
just a second, you pay per VM.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:22.740
You decide the shape of the VM,
how much CPU, memory, and disk

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.880
it has and pay for the
time it's reserved for you,

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.380
unlike the previous option,
which are based on usage.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.690
This can end up being
an economical option

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.820
if your compute usage
is a little more

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.740
on the consistent side.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.510
The same is true
of Google Compute

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:41.590
Engine, our final and most
flexible computing product.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.410
Why go with Google
Compute Engine instead?

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.590
Because sometimes you just
want a computer, whether that's

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.130
because you have existing
software or licensing reasons

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.680
or you need a specific
kernel, Compute Engine

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.200
gives you the VM to run
what you need to run there,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.463
all the way to a
custom OS image.

00:05:58.463 --> 00:06:00.880
This means that you can use
Compute Engine to run existing

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.890
applications in the
cloud without having

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:06.770
to make virtually any changes
to the software itself.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:10.117
So when you think of Compute
Engine, think high flexibility.

00:06:10.117 --> 00:06:11.950
With these quick
overviews, hopefully you're

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.200
feeling better about choosing
the right compute option

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.740
for your application.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:17.950
Remember that Google's
compute options are designed

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:20.800
to cover a range of use
cases and team structures,

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.560
so understanding exactly what
you need from Google Cloud

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.200
Platform is just as
important as understanding

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.050
what each of our compute
products does best.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.300
Check out our compute
options decision tree

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.580
at the Google Cloud
blog for more help.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.420
Thanks for watching,
and as always,

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:38.080
subscribe for more great Google
Cloud Platform video content.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:40.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

